    "result_text": "All 270 tests pass, including the new Cp09 test. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the sequential membership number rule for newly created owners.\n\n**The rule:** each owner's `membershipNumber` = one more than the number of owners that existed before it. Since a newly created owner always receives the largest auto-increment id, this equals `count(owners with id \u2264 this owner's id)` \u2014 which conveniently is stable when read back later (a value assigned at registration stays fixed even as more owners join).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipNumber` (`integer`, `int32`) property to the `Owner` schema, which regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field and setter.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a static helper `membershipNumber(Owner, Collection<Owner>)` computing the count-by-id, and marked the DTO target `ignore = true` so MapStruct leaves it for explicit population.\n\n3. **Responders** (`RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`) \u2014 inject `OwnerRepository`, and after mapping set `dto.setMembershipNumber(OwnerMapper.membershipNumber(owner, ownerRepository.findAll()))`. This covers the POST (created) response, the GET response the test reads back, and the PUT response for consistency.\n\nNo persistence/schema changes were needed \u2014 the number is derived deterministically from existing owner data, so it works identically across the JDBC/JPA/Spring-Data repository implementations.\n\nI chose to compute from `findAll()` (an existing interface method) rather than add a new count query, keeping the change confined to the mapping/response layer. This does load all owners per single-owner response; if the owner table were expected to grow very large, a dedicated `count(id \u2264 ?)` repository query would be more efficient \u2014 worth noting but not warranted here.",
